2. Aptitude / Math Questions
Q: If 35 was mistakenly entered as 53, and the resulting product increased by 540, what is the new product? Let the other multiplier be x. Correct product = 35x, mistaken product = 53x. Difference: 53x − 35x = 18x = 540 → x = 30. New (mistaken) product = 53 × 30 = 1590. (Correct product would've been 35 × 30 = 1050.)

Q: A product gets 25% off on day 1, then an additional 50% off the reduced price on day 2. Final price ₹60 — what was the original price? Let original = P. After day 1: 0.75P. After day 2: 0.5 × 0.75P = 0.375P. 0.375P = 60 → P = 160.

General tip: These are always "work backwards from the final value" problems — set up one variable, translate each sentence into an equation, solve last-to-first.

3. Pattern Program
Q: For n=3, print concentric square rings (n=3 example given: 5×5 grid)

33333
32223
32123
32223
33333
Logic: grid size = 2n−1. Each cell's value = n − (distance from nearest edge).

#include <stdio.h>

int min4(int a, int b, int c, int d) {
    int m = a;
    if (b < m) m = b;
    if (c < m) m = c;
    if (d < m) m = d;
    return m;
}

int main(void) {
    int n = 3;
    int size = 2 * n - 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < size; j++) {
            int dist_from_edge = min4(i, size - 1 - i, j, size - 1 - j);
            printf("%d", n - dist_from_edge);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
Explain while writing: outer ring is always n, and each ring inward decreases by 1, down to 1 at the center.

