Talking points: normalize to avoid redundancy (1NF/2NF/3NF), pick sensible primary/foreign keys, index columns you filter/join on frequently (e.g. manager_id), choose data types that match the domain (don't store money as float).

Q: How to delete duplicate entries in a table?

DELETE FROM employee
WHERE id NOT IN (
    SELECT MIN(id)
    FROM employee
    GROUP BY name, age
);
Explanation: groups rows by the columns that define a "duplicate" (name, age), keeps the lowest id in each group, deletes everything else. If the table has no unique key at all, you'd first add a surrogate key or use ROW_NUMBER() (SQL Server/Postgres):

DELETE FROM employee
WHERE id IN (
    SELECT id FROM (
        SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY name, age ORDER BY id) AS rn
        FROM employee
    ) t WHERE rn > 1
);
Q: How do you inspect which SQL query is taking time?

EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE on the query to see the execution plan — look for full table scans where an index should be used.
Enable the slow query log (MySQL: slow_query_log) to capture queries above a time threshold.
Check for missing indexes on WHERE/JOIN/ORDER BY columns.
Q: DB has multiple inserts/updates and is slow — how do you improve it?

Batch inserts (INSERT INTO t VALUES (...), (...), (...) instead of one INSERT per row).
Wrap the batch in a single transaction — committing per-row is expensive (disk sync on every commit).
Add indexes for lookups, but don't over-index — every index slows down inserts/updates.
Use prepared statements so the query isn't re-parsed/re-planned each time.
For very large loads, consider bulk-load utilities (LOAD DATA INFILE in MySQL) instead of row-by-row inserts.
5. Multithreading
Q: Explain multithreading, and what to watch for. Multiple threads share the same address space and run concurrently. Constraints to manage:

Race conditions — protect shared state with mutexes.
Deadlock — always acquire multiple locks in the same global order.
Oversubscription — don't spawn far more threads than CPU cores for CPU-bound work; use a thread pool.
Thread-unsafe library calls — e.g. strtok isn't thread-safe (strtok_r is); check any C library function you call.
When to use it: independent, parallelizable work, or I/O-bound tasks where threads can overlap waiting time (e.g. one thread waits on a socket while another processes data). When not to: the task is small enough that thread creation/sync overhead exceeds the benefit, or there's heavy contention on one shared resource anyway (serializes regardless of thread count).

Q: Explain lock and condition variables in C.

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
pthread_cond_t cond = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;
int ready = 0;

void *waiter(void *arg) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    while (!ready)
        pthread_cond_wait(&cond, &lock);   // releases lock while waiting, reacquires on wake
    printf("Condition met, proceeding\n");
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return NULL;
}

void *signaler(void *arg) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    ready = 1;
    pthread_cond_signal(&cond);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return NULL;
}
A mutex alone only protects data; a condition variable lets a thread sleep efficiently until some condition becomes true, instead of busy-polling. Always check the condition in a while loop (not if) — spurious wakeups are possible.

Q: What are "joins" (in a threading context)? pthread_join(thread_id, NULL) blocks the calling thread until the specified thread finishes — used to wait for worker threads to complete before the main thread proceeds (e.g., before reading their results or exiting).

pthread_t t1;
pthread_create(&t1, NULL, worker_fn, NULL);
// ... do other work ...
pthread_join(t1, NULL); // wait for t1 to finish
(If they instead meant SQL joins: INNER JOIN returns matching rows in both tables; LEFT JOIN returns all rows from the left table plus matches from the right, NULL where no match.)

Q: Explain race condition. Occurs when two or more threads access shared data concurrently, at least one writes, and the outcome depends on the timing/interleaving of execution. Classic example: counter++ is not atomic — it's really read, increment, write. If two threads interleave these three steps, one increment can be lost.

// UNSAFE without a lock:
counter++;   // read counter, add 1, write back — three separate steps
Fix: wrap in a mutex, or use atomic_int with atomic_fetch_add.

Q: Deadlock walkthrough — what happens in this code?

std::mutex m1, m2;

void Thread1() {
    m1.lock();
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));
    m2.lock();      // Thread1 now waits for m2
    m2.unlock();
    m1.unlock();
}

void Thread2() {
    m2.lock();
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));
    m1.lock();      // Thread2 now waits for m1
    m1.unlock();
    m2.unlock();
}
Thread1 holds m1 and waits for m2; Thread2 holds m2 and waits for m1. The 1-second sleep guarantees both threads are holding their first lock before trying for the second — classic deadlock (circular wait). Neither thread can ever proceed. Fix: always lock in the same global order in every thread (e.g. always m1 before m2), or use std::lock(m1, m2) which acquires both atomically without risk of this ordering issue.

6. Lock-Free Data Structures
Q: What are lock-free data structures, and why use them in multithreaded applications? Structures where threads coordinate using atomic hardware instructions (like compare-and-swap) instead of mutexes, so no thread ever blocks another. Used because:

Avoids context-switch and priority-inversion costs of locks.
No risk of deadlock (no locks to circularly wait on).
Better throughput/latency under high contention — important in low-latency systems (trading engines, real-time monitoring, which is close to your eBPF background).
#include <stdatomic.h>
atomic_int counter = 0;

void safe_increment(void) {
    int old_val, new_val;
    do {
        old_val = atomic_load(&counter);
        new_val = old_val + 1;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&counter, &old_val, new_val));
}
Trade-off to mention: harder to implement correctly (ABA problem, memory ordering), so used selectively — usually in hot paths, not everywhere.

7. Client-Server / Socket Programming
Q: In a multi-client server, how does the server identify which client sent data? Each accept() call returns a distinct socket file descriptor for that connection. The server keeps a map/array from fd → client state (client ID, buffer, session info). When select/poll/epoll reports a ready fd, the server looks up that fd's associated client struct — the fd itself is the identity.

Q: How does a server continuously monitor and receive from multiple clients? Register every client socket (non-blocking) with epoll (Linux) or select/poll. The event loop blocks in epoll_wait, which returns only the fds that actually have data — the server then reads just those, so no client stalls another.

struct epoll_event events[MAX_EVENTS];
while (1) {
    int n = epoll_wait(epfd, events, MAX_EVENTS, -1);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int fd = events[i].data.fd;
        if (fd == listen_fd) {
            int client_fd = accept(listen_fd, NULL, NULL);
            make_nonblocking(client_fd);
            struct epoll_event cev = {.events = EPOLLIN, .data.fd = client_fd};
            epoll_ctl(epfd, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, client_fd, &cev);
        } else {
            char buf[4096];
            ssize_t n_read = recv(fd, buf, sizeof(buf), 0);
            if (n_read <= 0) {
                epoll_ctl(epfd, EPOLL_CTL_DEL, fd, NULL);
                close(fd);
            } else {
                // process buf, feed into that client's message-assembly state
            }
        }
    }
}
Q: How would you handle partial/incomplete TCP messages? TCP is a byte stream — one recv() may return only part of a logical message. Fix with length-prefix framing: send a fixed-size header with the payload length, buffer bytes per-client until the full length is received.

typedef struct {
    uint8_t header[4];
    size_t header_have;
    uint8_t *payload;
    uint32_t payload_len;
    size_t payload_have;
} client_msg_state;

int feed_data(client_msg_state *s, const uint8_t *data, size_t len) {
    size_t off = 0;
    while (off < len) {
        if (s->header_have < 4) {
            size_t need = 4 - s->header_have;
            size_t take = (len - off < need) ? (len - off) : need;
            memcpy(s->header + s->header_have, data + off, take);
            s->header_have += take; off += take;
            if (s->header_have == 4) {
                s->payload_len = ntohl(*(uint32_t *)s->header);
                s->payload = malloc(s->payload_len);
                s->payload_have = 0;
            }
            continue;
        }
        size_t need = s->payload_len - s->payload_have;
        size_t take = (len - off < need) ? (len - off) : need;
        memcpy(s->payload + s->payload_have, data + off, take);
        s->payload_have += take; off += take;
        if (s->payload_have == s->payload_len) return 1; // message complete
    }
    return 0; // wait for more data
}
Also mention: validate the length header before malloc-ing (cap it) so a malicious/corrupt client can't force a huge allocation.

Q: Practical experience with socket programming / file transfer? Be ready to describe: opening a listening socket, accept() loop, sending a file in fixed-size chunks with send()/read() loops (handling partial writes the same way as partial reads), and optionally a checksum (CRC32/MD5) to verify integrity after transfer — tie this to your FIM project's MD5 usage if relevant.

8. Pointers, Memory Management & Structs in C
Q: Why are pointers important in C? Where would you use them? They let you: work directly with memory addresses (needed for dynamic allocation), pass large structures to functions by reference (avoid copying), build linked data structures (linked lists, trees, hash maps), and implement callbacks via function pointers. Central to systems programming — most of your OS/kernel-adjacent work depends on pointer manipulation.

Q: Represent employee records fetched from a DB, in C.

typedef struct {
    int id;
    char name[64];
    float salary;
} Employee;

Employee *employees = malloc(100 * sizeof(Employee));
int count = 0;
// fill via DB fetch loop:
// employees[count].id = ...; strcpy(employees[count].name, ...); count++;
Q: You allocated space for 100 records but got more — how do you handle it?

if (count == capacity) {
    capacity *= 2;
    Employee *tmp = realloc(employees, capacity * sizeof(Employee));
    if (tmp == NULL) {
        // realloc failed — original block is untouched, still valid
        perror("realloc failed");
        // handle gracefully: stop, or free(employees) and abort
    } else {
        employees = tmp;
    }
}
Key point interviewers want: never write employees = realloc(employees, ...) directly — if realloc fails and returns NULL, you lose the pointer to the original (still-valid) block, causing a memory leak.

Q: malloc() vs calloc() vs realloc()?

malloc(size) — allocates uninitialized memory.
calloc(n, size) — allocates n * size bytes, zero-initialized, also guards against multiplication overflow internally.
realloc(ptr, new_size) — resizes a previous allocation, preserving existing content up to the smaller of old/new size; may move the block.
Q: Precautions with realloc()? Always assign to a temporary pointer first (see above), check for NULL on failure, and remember the pointer may have moved — don't keep old pointers/pointers-into-the-old-block around after a successful realloc.

Q: What if realloc() fails? It returns NULL and the original block remains valid and unchanged. Your code must check for this and decide: retry with a smaller size, free the original and report an error, or abort — but never silently overwrite your only pointer to the original data.

