9. String Handling
Q: How would you split a string like "Raghav_Chopra" using _ as the delimiter?

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    char str[] = "Raghav_Chopra";
    char *token = strtok(str, "_");
    while (token != NULL) {
        printf("%s\n", token);
        token = strtok(NULL, "_");
    }
    return 0;
}
Q: Count words with a specific delimiter.

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    char str[100];
    scanf("%[^\n]", str);
    char *word = strtok(str, " ,.-");
    while (word != NULL) {
        printf("%s = %lu\n", word, strlen(word));
        word = strtok(NULL, " ,.-");
    }
    return 0;
}
Worth mentioning: strtok modifies the original string in place (inserts \0 at each delimiter) and keeps internal state, so it's not thread-safe or reentrant. strtok_r takes an explicit saveptr argument and is the safe alternative in multithreaded code.

10. File Processing at Scale
Q: Given a huge file of EmployeeID, ManagerID, generate ManagerID → count of reports. Stream the file line by line (don't load it all into memory), and accumulate counts in a hash map keyed by manager ID.

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "uthash.h"   // common C hash table library

typedef struct {
    int manager_id;
    int count;
    UT_hash_handle hh;
} ManagerCount;

int main(void) {
    FILE *f = fopen("employees.txt", "r");
    ManagerCount *counts = NULL;
    int emp_id, mgr_id;

    while (fscanf(f, "%d,%d", &emp_id, &mgr_id) == 2) {
        ManagerCount *entry;
        HASH_FIND_INT(counts, &mgr_id, entry);
        if (!entry) {
            entry = malloc(sizeof(ManagerCount));
            entry->manager_id = mgr_id;
            entry->count = 0;
            HASH_ADD_INT(counts, manager_id, entry);
        }
        entry->count++;
    }
    fclose(f);

    FILE *out = fopen("manager_counts.txt", "w");
    ManagerCount *cur, *tmp;
    HASH_ITER(hh, counts, cur, tmp) {
        fprintf(out, "%d,%d\n", cur->manager_id, cur->count);
    }
    fclose(out);
    return 0;
}
This is the same pattern for: "trades per trading manager" and "products per category" — stream, hash-map-aggregate, write once at the end.

Q: How do you check whether a Manager ID already exists while processing?

Hash table (as above) — average O(1) lookup, best choice for streaming data.
If memory is very constrained: keep a sorted array and use binary search — O(log n) lookup, but O(n) insert unless you batch-sort at the end.
Q: If using linear search, how do you improve average lookup performance? Sort the data once and binary-search (O(log n) instead of O(n)), or switch to a hash table for O(1) average lookup — hashing is almost always the better answer for this exact scenario.

Q: Big file with product_name, category — read efficiently, return total products per category. Same streaming + hash-map-by-category pattern as above; read with a buffered fgets/fscanf loop rather than reading the whole file into memory, so the memory footprint stays proportional to the number of distinct categories, not file size.

11. Segmentation Faults
Q: How do you detect and solve a segfault?

Reproduce under gdb, get a backtrace (bt) at the crash point.
Use AddressSanitizer (-fsanitize=address) or Valgrind to catch invalid memory access (use-after-free, buffer overflow, out-of-bounds) — often before it actually crashes.
Check for classic causes: NULL pointer dereference, use-after-free, buffer overflow, uninitialized pointer, stack overflow from deep/unbounded recursion.
Q: How can you detect it even when the program runs fine and gives no error? This describes silent memory corruption — the invalid write lands in memory that isn't "hot" yet, so no immediate crash. Detect it with AddressSanitizer/Valgrind (they flag the invalid access itself, not just eventual visible corruption), add bounds-checking/assertions in debug builds, and run under varied loads/inputs — heap layout differences can expose the bug that a "lucky" layout hides.

Q: Real situation where a segfault can occur? Use-after-free (freeing a struct, then a stale pointer elsewhere still writes to it later); writing past the end of a stack buffer, corrupting the return address; dereferencing a pointer that was never initialized.

12. Performance / Optimization
Q: An EOD task takes 30 minutes — how do you optimize it?

Profile first — find where the time actually goes (don't guess).
If it's DB-bound: batch inserts/updates into single transactions, add appropriate indexes, avoid N+1 query patterns.
If it's CPU-bound and the work is parallelizable: split across threads/processes.
If it's I/O-bound: use buffered/async I/O, avoid opening/closing files or connections repeatedly.
Q: How would you optimize latency (general)? Profile to find the actual bottleneck; reduce lock contention (shrink critical sections, consider lock-free structures for hot paths); batch small I/O operations; cache repeated lookups; avoid unnecessary memory allocation/copies in hot paths; for a server, prefer epoll over thread-per-connection at scale.

Q: We're seeing delays in bulk operations — how do you find the cause? Add timing/logging around each stage (read → parse → transform → write) to isolate which stage is slow, check DB slow-query logs, and use a profiler (perf, valgrind --tool=callgrind) to find hot functions rather than guessing.

