private:
    int x = 10;
public:
    int GetX() { return x; }
};
Test t;
cout << t.x;   // ERROR
Compilation error — x is private, inaccessible outside the class. Must go through the public accessor: cout << t.GetX();.

Q: What's wrong with this code?

class Employee {
    int* salary;
public:
    Employee() { salary = new int(5000); }
};
No destructor — the memory allocated with new is never freed → memory leak. Also violates the Rule of Three: no copy constructor or copy-assignment operator defined, so copying an Employee does a shallow copy of the pointer — two objects end up pointing at the same int, and when both are destroyed (once a destructor is added), you get a double free. Fix: add a destructor (delete salary;), and either define proper copy semantics or (better in modern C++) use std::unique_ptr<int> instead of a raw pointer.

Q: Public/private/protected access specifiers, and public/private keys.

private — accessible only inside the class.
protected — accessible in the class and derived classes.
public — accessible from anywhere. If they meant cryptographic keys (plausible in a fintech context): public-key (asymmetric) cryptography uses a public key to encrypt/verify and a private key to decrypt/sign — used for TLS handshakes, digital signatures on transactions, etc.
