16. Additional Likely Follow-Up Questions
Interviewers almost always probe one level deeper than the original question. These are natural follow-ups to what's already in this sheet — prep them too.

C / Memory
Difference between stack and heap memory? Stack: fast, fixed-size, auto-freed on function return, used for local variables. Heap: manually managed (malloc/free), larger, used for data that must outlive the function or whose size isn't known at compile time.
What is a memory leak, and how do you detect one? Allocated memory never freed. Detect with Valgrind (--leak-check=full) or AddressSanitizer's leak detector; in long-running servers, watch RSS growth over time as a symptom.
Difference between free() and setting a pointer to NULL? free() releases the memory but the pointer still holds the old (now invalid) address — a dangling pointer — until you explicitly set it to NULL. Best practice: free(p); p = NULL; to avoid accidental use-after-free.
What is a dangling pointer vs a wild pointer? Dangling: pointed to valid memory that's since been freed. Wild: never initialized, points to garbage.
Difference between const char *, char * const, and const char * const? First: pointer to constant data (can't modify the chars, can reassign the pointer). Second: constant pointer to mutable data (can modify chars, can't reassign pointer). Third: both are constant.
What is a memory alignment / padding issue in structs? Compilers insert padding bytes so struct members align to their natural boundaries (e.g., an int on a 4-byte boundary) — can make sizeof(struct) larger than the sum of member sizes. Reorder members largest-to-smallest to minimize padding if size matters.
Multithreading / Concurrency
Difference between concurrency and parallelism? Concurrency: multiple tasks make progress by interleaving (even on one core). Parallelism: tasks literally execute at the same instant (needs multiple cores).
What is a spinlock, and when would you use one over a mutex? Busy-waits instead of sleeping — cheaper than a mutex if the critical section is very short (avoids context-switch overhead), but wastes CPU if held long. Used in kernel/low-latency code where lock hold time is a few instructions.
What is false sharing? Two threads modify different variables that happen to sit on the same CPU cache line, causing unnecessary cache invalidation traffic between cores even though there's no logical data race. Fixed by padding/aligning hot variables to separate cache lines.
What is priority inversion? A low-priority thread holds a lock a high-priority thread needs, and a medium-priority thread preempts the low-priority one — the high-priority thread is indirectly blocked by the medium one. Fixed with priority inheritance protocols.
How would you implement a reader-writer lock? Allow multiple concurrent readers but exclusive access for a writer — pthread_rwlock_t in C, or build one with a mutex + condition variable + reader count.
How do you make a function thread-safe? Avoid shared mutable state where possible (prefer local variables); if state must be shared, protect it with a mutex; avoid non-reentrant library calls (strtok → strtok_r, localtime → localtime_r).
Sockets / Networking
TCP vs UDP — when would you choose each? TCP: reliable, ordered, connection-oriented — for anything where lost/out-of-order data breaks correctness (a financial transaction, a file transfer). UDP: no ordering/delivery guarantees but lower overhead — used for real-time data where a dropped packet is acceptable (video/audio streaming, some market-data feeds).
What is the TCP three-way handshake? SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK — establishes a connection and synchronizes sequence numbers before any data is sent.
What is Nagle's algorithm, and why might you disable it (TCP_NODELAY)? Nagle's batches small outgoing packets to reduce network overhead, but this adds latency — for low-latency systems (trading, interactive protocols) you disable it with setsockopt(fd, IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_NODELAY, ...) so small messages go out immediately.
What does SO_REUSEADDR do and why is it commonly set on a listening socket? Lets you rebind to a port still in TIME_WAIT from a previous instance of the server — avoids "address already in use" errors on quick restarts.
How do you handle a client that disconnects unexpectedly? recv() returning 0 means an orderly close; a negative return with errno other than EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK means an error — either way, deregister the fd from epoll and close() it, and clean up any per-client state.
How would you scale this server beyond one machine? Load balancer in front of multiple server instances, with client "stickiness" or shared session state (e.g., in Redis) if a client's state needs to be visible across instances.
SQL / Database
Difference between INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, RIGHT JOIN, FULL OUTER JOIN? Inner: only matching rows in both tables. Left: all left-table rows, matched right-table columns or NULL. Right: mirror of left. Full outer: all rows from both sides, NULL where no match.
What is an index, and what's the trade-off of adding one? A separate data structure (usually B-tree) that speeds up lookups/sorts on a column, at the cost of extra storage and slower writes (the index must be updated on every insert/update/delete).
What is a transaction isolation level? Controls what concurrent transactions can see of each other's uncommitted/committed changes — READ UNCOMMITTED < READ COMMITTED < REPEATABLE READ < SERIALIZABLE, trading concurrency for consistency guarantees.
What is a deadlock in a database, and how does the DB handle it? Two transactions each hold a lock the other needs. Most DBs detect this automatically and abort one transaction (deadlock victim) so the other can proceed — your application should catch that error and retry.
Normalization vs denormalization — when would you denormalize? Normalize to eliminate redundancy/anomalies; denormalize (accept some redundancy) when read performance matters more than write simplicity — e.g., precomputing a report_count column instead of a JOIN+COUNT on every read.
OOP / C++
Difference between overloading and overriding? Overloading: same function name, different parameter list, resolved at compile time. Overriding: a derived class redefines a virtual function from the base class, resolved at runtime via the vtable.
What is a virtual function, and what is a pure virtual function? Virtual: allows a derived class to override behavior, dispatched at runtime. Pure virtual (= 0): no implementation in the base class, forces derived classes to implement it, makes the base class abstract (can't be instantiated).
What is the Rule of Three / Rule of Five in C++? If a class manages a raw resource (like the Employee/salary example above) and needs a custom destructor, it almost always also needs a custom copy constructor and copy-assignment operator (Rule of Three); modern C++ adds move constructor and move-assignment (Rule of Five) for efficient transfers.
Difference between struct and class in C++? Only difference is default access: struct members are public by default, class members are private by default.
Aptitude / Logic (likely more word problems)
Ratio/percentage problems: practice translating "X% more/less than Y" and "successive discounts" into equations quickly — that's the pattern behind both examples already in this sheet.
Speed/time/work problems: classic fintech-adjacent aptitude staple — e.g., "A can do a job in X days, B in Y days, working together how long?" (1/X + 1/Y = 1/total_days).
Simple/compound interest: given it's fintech, brush up A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt) and simple interest I = PRT/100 — plausible given the domain.
Quick Reference — What Chella Seems To Emphasize
