LINUX/UNIX SYSTEMS PROGRAMMING - QUICK REFERENCE
(Kept brief - JD doesn't explicitly call out these topics by name,
so know them at this level, don't over-prepare deep internals.)
(Type | One-line explanation | One-line code)
================================================================

1. PROCESS vs THREAD, FORK/EXEC
================================================================
Process                | Independent program in execution with its own isolated memory space.
                       | pid_t pid = getpid();

Thread                 | Lightweight execution unit sharing memory with other threads in the same process.
                       | pthread_create(&tid, NULL, func, NULL);

fork()                 | Creates a near-identical copy of the calling process (child).
                       | pid_t pid = fork();

exec() family          | Replaces the current process image with a new program.
                       | execl("/bin/ls", "ls", NULL);

fork() + exec()        | Common pattern: child forks, then replaces itself with a new program.
                       | if (fork() == 0) execl("/bin/ls", "ls", NULL);

wait()/waitpid()       | Parent blocks until a child process finishes, to reap it.
                       | waitpid(pid, &status, 0);

Zombie Process         | Child that finished but parent hasn't called wait() yet.
                       | // occurs if parent skips waitpid()

Orphan Process         | Child whose parent exited before it did; reparented to init.
                       | // occurs if parent dies first


2. IPC MECHANISMS
================================================================
Pipe                   | One-way byte stream between related (parent-child) processes.
                       | pipe(fd);   // fd[0]=read, fd[1]=write

Named Pipe (FIFO)      | Pipe with a filesystem path, usable by unrelated processes.
                       | mkfifo("/tmp/myfifo", 0666);

Unix Domain Socket     | Local bidirectional IPC using a filesystem path, supports FD passing.
                       | socket(AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM, 0);

Shared Memory          | Fastest IPC - multiple processes map the same memory region directly.
                       | shmget(key, size, IPC_CREAT | 0666);

Message Queue          | Kernel-managed queue of discrete, typed messages between processes.
                       | msgget(key, IPC_CREAT | 0666);

Signals (as IPC)       | Simple async notification between processes (e.g., kill a process).
                       | kill(pid, SIGTERM);


3. MULTITHREADING
================================================================
Mutex                  | Lock ensuring only one thread accesses a resource at a time.
                       | pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

Semaphore              | Counter-based lock allowing N threads concurrent access.
                       | sem_wait(&sem);

Race Condition         | Bug where outcome depends on unpredictable thread scheduling order.
                       | counter++;   // unsafe if unprotected across threads

Deadlock               | Two+ threads waiting on locks the other holds - permanent stall.
                       | // Thread A: lock(L1) then lock(L2); Thread B: lock(L2) then lock(L1)

Condition Variable     | Lets a thread sleep until another thread signals a condition is true.
                       | pthread_cond_wait(&cond, &lock);

Thread Join            | Main/caller thread waits for a spawned thread to finish.
                       | pthread_join(tid, NULL);

False Sharing          | Threads updating different vars on the same cache line slow each other down.
                       | // fix: pad struct so hot vars land on separate cache lines


4. FILE I/O INTERNALS, FILE DESCRIPTORS
================================================================
File Descriptor        | Integer handle the OS uses to reference an open file/socket/pipe.
                       | int fd = open("file.txt", O_RDONLY);

open()                 | Opens a file and returns a file descriptor.
                       | int fd = open("file.txt", O_RDWR);

read()/write()         | Low-level syscalls to read/write bytes via a file descriptor.
                       | read(fd, buf, sizeof(buf));

close()                | Releases a file descriptor back to the OS.
                       | close(fd);

lseek()                | Moves the read/write offset within an open file.
                       | lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_SET);

Standard FDs           | 0 = stdin, 1 = stdout, 2 = stderr - always open by default.
                       | write(1, "hi\n", 3);   // writes to stdout


5. SIGNAL HANDLING
================================================================
Signal                 | Async notification sent to a process (e.g., interrupt, crash, terminate).
                       | kill(pid, SIGINT);

signal()/sigaction()   | Registers a custom handler function for a given signal.
                       | signal(SIGINT, handler);

SIGSEGV                | Sent on invalid memory access (segmentation fault).
                       | // e.g., dereferencing NULL triggers SIGSEGV

SIGTERM                | Polite request asking a process to terminate gracefully.
                       | kill(pid, SIGTERM);

SIGKILL                | Forcefully kills a process immediately - cannot be caught/ignored.
                       | kill(pid, SIGKILL);

Async-Signal-Safe      | Rule: signal handlers should do minimal work (e.g., set a flag only).
                       | volatile sig_atomic_t flag = 0;  // set inside handler


6. SYSTEM CALLS vs LIBRARY CALLS
================================================================
System Call            | Direct request to the kernel (e.g., read, write, fork) - causes a context switch.
                       | ssize_t n = read(fd, buf, size);

Library Call           | Higher-level function in a library (e.g., libc) that may wrap a syscall.
                       | fread(buf, 1, size, filePtr);   // wraps read() internally

Key Difference         | Syscalls cross into kernel mode (slower); library calls run in user space unless they wrap a syscall.
                       | printf() -> buffers in user space -> eventually calls write() syscall

