1. GDB - BREAKPOINTS, WATCHPOINTS, CORE DUMP ANALYSIS
================================================================
Start GDB                | Launches GDB on a compiled binary for debugging.
                         | gdb ./myprogram

Breakpoint               | Pauses execution at a specific line/function.
                         | break main

Run                      | Starts program execution inside GDB.
                         | run

Next                     | Executes the next line, stepping OVER function calls.
                         | next

Step                     | Executes the next line, stepping INTO function calls.
                         | step

Continue                 | Resumes execution until the next breakpoint.
                         | continue

Watchpoint                | Pauses execution whenever a variable's value changes.
                         | watch myVar

Print                    | Displays the current value of a variable.
                         | print myVar

Backtrace                | Shows the call stack at the current point of execution.
                         | bt

Enable Core Dumps        | Allows the OS to save program memory state on crash.
                         | ulimit -c unlimited

Analyze Core Dump         | Loads a crashed program's core dump for post-mortem analysis.
                         | gdb ./myprogram core

Thread Backtrace (all)     | Shows call stacks of all threads - useful for deadlock analysis.
                         | thread apply all bt


2. VALGRIND - MEMORY LEAK DETECTION
================================================================
Run Valgrind (memcheck)    | Detects memory leaks and invalid memory access.
                         | valgrind --leak-check=full ./myprogram

Leak Summary              | Reports how much memory was leaked and where it was allocated.
                         | // shown automatically at program exit under valgrind

Invalid Read/Write        | Flags access to memory outside allocated bounds.
                         | // valgrind reports "Invalid write of size X"

Uninitialized Value Use    | Flags use of a variable before it was assigned a value.
                         | // valgrind reports "Conditional jump depends on uninitialised value"

Limitation                | Valgrind slows execution heavily, so it's poor for catching race conditions (timing-based bugs).
                         | // use ThreadSanitizer (-fsanitize=thread) for races instead


3. DEBUGGING A CRASH/SEGFAULT WITH NO REPRO STEPS
================================================================
Step 1: Enable Core Dump   | Make sure a core dump is captured next time it crashes.
                         | ulimit -c unlimited

Step 2: Analyze Core        | Load the dump in GDB to see exact crash location and state.
                         | gdb ./myprogram core

Step 3: Check Common Causes | Look for null deref, out-of-bounds access, or use-after-free first.
                         | print *ptr   // check if ptr is NULL/garbage

Step 4: Run Under Valgrind  | Reproduce under Valgrind if possible to catch memory corruption earlier than the actual crash point.
                         | valgrind ./myprogram

Step 5: Add Logging         | If intermittent (suspected race condition), add logging around suspected shared state.
                         | fprintf(stderr, "state=%d\n", state);

Step 6: Check Recent Changes | Correlate crash onset with recent code/config changes as a hypothesis source.
                         | git log --since="1 week ago"


4. ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS (RCA) APPROACH
================================================================
Reproduce                | Try to reliably trigger the issue in a safe/test environment first.
                         | // run the same input/load that triggered production issue

Gather Evidence           | Collect logs, core dumps, and metrics from the actual incident.
                         | tail -n 500 /var/log/myapp.log

Form Hypothesis           | Propose a likely cause based on evidence and recent changes.
                         | // e.g., "likely a race condition introduced in last deploy"

Validate Hypothesis        | Test the hypothesis with a targeted, isolated test - don't guess-fix.
                         | // write a minimal repro test case

Fix Root Cause             | Fix the underlying issue, not just the visible symptom.
                         | // e.g., add proper locking, not just a retry wrapper

Add Regression Safeguard   | Add a test or monitoring/alert so the same bug is caught earlier next time.
                         | // add unit test + log-based alert for the condition

Document                 | Write up cause, fix, and prevention for the team (postmortem).
                         | // short RCA doc: what happened, why, fix, prevention

