DATABASE / SQL - QUICK REFERENCE
(Explicitly called out in JD - "Very good knowledge of database
concepts... SQL, relational algebra, and query optimization" -
know this section well.)
(Type | One-line explanation | One-line code)
================================================================

1. JOINS
================================================================
INNER JOIN              | Returns only rows with matching values in both tables.
                        | SELECT * FROM A INNER JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;

LEFT JOIN               | Returns all rows from left table, matched rows from right (NULL if no match).
                        | SELECT * FROM A LEFT JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;

RIGHT JOIN              | Returns all rows from right table, matched rows from left (NULL if no match).
                        | SELECT * FROM A RIGHT JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;

FULL OUTER JOIN         | Returns all rows from both tables, NULLs where no match on either side.
                        | SELECT * FROM A FULL OUTER JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;

SELF JOIN               | Joins a table to itself, usually to compare rows within the same table.
                        | SELECT a1.name FROM Emp a1, Emp a2 WHERE a1.mgr_id = a2.id;

CROSS JOIN              | Returns the Cartesian product - every row of A paired with every row of B.
                        | SELECT * FROM A CROSS JOIN B;


2. INDEXES
================================================================
Index                  | Data structure (usually B-tree) that speeds up lookups on a column.
                        | CREATE INDEX idx_name ON Employees(name);

Primary Key Index      | Automatically created unique index enforcing row identity.
                        | CREATE TABLE T (id INT PRIMARY KEY);

Unique Index            | Ensures no duplicate values exist in the indexed column.
                        | CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_email ON Users(email);

Composite Index         | Index built on multiple columns together, ordered left-to-right.
                        | CREATE INDEX idx_name_dept ON Employees(name, dept);

Clustered Index          | Determines the physical storage order of table rows (usually the PK).
                        | -- e.g., PRIMARY KEY often creates the clustered index by default

Non-Clustered Index      | Separate structure pointing to row locations; table order unaffected.
                        | CREATE INDEX idx_dept ON Employees(dept);   -- non-clustered by default

Index Tradeoff           | Speeds up reads (SELECT) but slows down writes (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE).
                        | -- every write must also update the index structure


3. QUERY OPTIMIZATION BASICS
================================================================
EXPLAIN Plan             | Shows how the database engine will execute a query (scan type, index use).
                        | EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM Employees WHERE dept = 'IT';

Full Table Scan          | Engine reads every row - slow, usually means a missing/unused index.
                        | -- happens when WHERE column has no index

Avoid SELECT *           | Fetch only needed columns to reduce I/O and network transfer.
                        | SELECT name, dept FROM Employees;   -- not SELECT *

WHERE Filter Order       | Put the most selective condition first to reduce rows early.
                        | WHERE dept = 'IT' AND status = 'active';

Avoid Functions on Indexed Columns | Wrapping an indexed column in a function prevents index use.
                        | -- BAD: WHERE YEAR(join_date) = 2024 (skips index)

Query Caching            | Reusing results of a previously executed identical query.
                        | -- handled by DB engine automatically for repeated identical queries

N+1 Query Problem         | Running one query per row in a loop instead of one batched query - major performance killer.
                        | -- fix: use a single JOIN instead of a query-per-row loop


4. NORMALIZATION
================================================================
1NF (First Normal Form)   | Each column holds atomic (indivisible) values, no repeating groups.
                        | -- BAD: phone = "123,456"  → split into separate rows/columns

2NF (Second Normal Form)  | 1NF + no partial dependency (non-key column depends on part of a composite key).
                        | -- move data depending only on part of the key into its own table

3NF (Third Normal Form)   | 2NF + no transitive dependency (non-key column depends on another non-key column).
                        | -- e.g., move "city depends on zip" into a separate ZipCode table

Denormalization           | Intentionally adding redundancy to speed up reads, trading off write complexity.
                        | -- e.g., storing total_amount directly on Orders instead of recalculating via JOIN

Foreign Key                | Column referencing the primary key of another table, enforcing referential integrity.
                        | FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES Departments(id);


5. RELATIONAL ALGEBRA CONCEPTS
================================================================
Selection (σ)             | Filters rows based on a condition - maps to SQL WHERE.
                        | σ(dept='IT')(Employees)   →  SQL: WHERE dept='IT'

Projection (π)             | Selects specific columns - maps to SQL SELECT column list.
                        | π(name, dept)(Employees)  →  SQL: SELECT name, dept

Union (∪)                  | Combines rows from two compatible relations, removing duplicates.
                        | A ∪ B   →  SQL: SELECT * FROM A UNION SELECT * FROM B;

Set Difference (−)          | Rows in one relation but not in another.
                        | A − B   →  SQL: SELECT * FROM A EXCEPT SELECT * FROM B;

Cartesian Product (×)        | Every row of one relation paired with every row of another.
                        | A × B   →  SQL: SELECT * FROM A CROSS JOIN B;

Join (⋈)                   | Combines rows from two relations based on a related column.
                        | A ⋈ B   →  SQL: SELECT * FROM A JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;

================================================================
