SECTION 1: C LANGUAGE FUNDAMENTALS
================================================================

Q1: Explain pointers and pointer arithmetic.
A: A pointer holds the memory address of a variable. Pointer arithmetic moves
by the size of the pointed-to type, not by raw bytes — e.g., int* p; p+1
advances 4 bytes on most systems, not 1. This matters when walking arrays or
buffers manually, which is common in low-level protocol/message parsing code.

Q2: What is a function pointer and where would you use one?
A: A function pointer stores the address of a function so it can be called
indirectly. Common uses: callback mechanisms, dispatch tables (e.g., mapping
message types to handler functions in a trading engine), and plugin-style
architectures where behavior is selected at runtime instead of compile time.

Q3: Pointer-to-pointer — when is it needed?
A: Used when a function needs to modify the caller's pointer itself, not just
the data it points to — e.g., a function that allocates memory and needs to
hand the new address back via an output parameter: void alloc(int** out).

Q4: Explain malloc/free and common memory bugs.
A: malloc reserves heap memory; free releases it back. Common bugs: memory
leaks (forgetting free), dangling pointers (using memory after free), and
double-free (freeing the same pointer twice, which corrupts heap metadata).
In performance-critical systems, excessive malloc/free calls also hurt
latency, so many low-latency codebases pre-allocate or use memory pools.

Q5: Structs vs unions, and what are bit-fields?
A: A struct allocates separate memory for each member; a union overlays all
members on the same memory, so only one is valid at a time — useful for
memory-efficient variant types. Bit-fields let you pack multiple small values
into specific bit-widths within an int, which is common when parsing tightly
packed binary protocol headers (e.g., FIX/ITCH-style market data messages).

Q6: What does 'volatile' do, and when do you need it?
A: 'volatile' tells the compiler not to optimize away or cache reads/writes
to a variable, because its value can change outside the program's normal
flow — e.g., a variable modified by a signal handler, another thread, or
memory-mapped hardware. Without it, the compiler might cache a stale value
in a register.

Q7: const vs static — practical difference.
A: const means the value can't be modified after initialization (compile-time
enforced immutability). static, on a local variable, makes it persist across
function calls (retains value); on a global/function, it restricts linkage to
the current file (internal linkage) — useful for hiding helper functions.

Q8: Stack vs heap — how do you choose?
A: Stack: fast, automatic cleanup, limited size, scope-bound (local vars).
Heap: manual management, larger capacity, needed when data must outlive the
function or size isn't known at compile time. In low-latency code, heavy
stack use is often preferred because heap allocation is comparatively slow
and unpredictable (fragmentation, allocator locks).


SECTION 2: LINUX / UNIX SYSTEMS PROGRAMMING
================================================================

Q9: Process vs thread — key differences.
A: A process has its own isolated memory space; threads within a process
share the same memory space (heap, globals) but have separate stacks.
Threads are cheaper to create and communicate faster (shared memory) but
require synchronization to avoid data races. Processes are safer/isolated
but communication needs IPC.

Q10: Explain fork() and exec().
A: fork() creates a near-identical copy of the calling process (child gets
a copy of the parent's memory, same code, separate PID). exec() replaces
the current process image with a new program. The classic pattern is
fork() then exec() in the child — used by shells to launch new programs
while the parent continues running.

Q11: What IPC mechanisms do you know, and when would you pick each?
A: Pipes — simple parent-child byte stream, low setup cost. Unix domain
sockets — bidirectional, supports multiple clients, can pass file
descriptors, good for local daemon-to-client communication (I used this in
my 2FA-for-SSH project). Shared memory — fastest, avoids copying data
between processes, best for high-throughput/low-latency needs like market
data feeds. Message queues — structured, discrete messages with built-in
queuing. Choice depends on throughput needs, number of peers, and whether
data needs to be copied or shared directly.

Q12: Explain mutex vs semaphore.
A: A mutex enforces mutual exclusion — only one thread can hold it at a
time, and typically only the thread that locked it can unlock it (ownership
semantics). A semaphore is a counter that allows N threads through
simultaneously; it doesn't have ownership, so any thread can signal it.
Use a mutex to protect a shared resource; use a semaphore to limit
concurrent access to a pool of N resources (e.g., N worker slots).

Q13: What is deadlock, and how do you avoid it?
A: Deadlock happens when two or more threads each hold a lock the other
needs, so neither can proceed — classic case: Thread A holds Lock1 and
waits for Lock2, Thread B holds Lock2 and waits for Lock1. Avoidance:
always acquire locks in a fixed global order, use timeouts (trylock), or
minimize the number of locks held simultaneously.

Q14: What is a race condition, and how do condition variables help?
A: A race condition occurs when the outcome depends on unpredictable
thread scheduling — e.g., two threads incrementing a shared counter
without synchronization, causing lost updates. Condition variables let a
thread sleep until a specific condition becomes true (signaled by another
thread), avoiding busy-waiting — used together with a mutex to safely
check and wait on shared state (e.g., a producer-consumer queue).

Q15: What is false sharing?
A: When two threads modify different variables that happen to sit on the
same CPU cache line, each write invalidates the other core's cached copy,
causing unnecessary cache-coherency traffic and slowing both threads down —
even though there's no actual logical data conflict. Fix: pad/align
shared structures so hot variables used by different threads land on
separate cache lines. This is a real concern in low-latency trading code.

Q16: What are signals, and how do you handle them safely?
A: Signals are asynchronous notifications delivered to a process (e.g.,
SIGINT, SIGSEGV, SIGTERM). A signal handler should do minimal, async-signal-
safe work (e.g., just set a flag) rather than calling non-reentrant
functions like malloc or printf inside the handler, since the signal can
interrupt the program at any point, including mid-allocation.


